    <comment>New package: qdmr 0.15.1, GPL-3.0-or-later.

qDMR is a graphical codeplug programming tool (CPS) for DMR amateur
radios: it reads, edits, verifies and writes the codeplug (channels,
zones, contacts, scan lists, callsign DB) of the radio over USB. It
ships the qdmr GUI, the dmrconf command line tool, and libdmrconf,
split per the shared library policy into libdmrconf0 and qdmr-devel.

Supported radios include Radioddity/Baofeng RD-5R and GD-77, TYT
MD-UV390/MD-2017, Retevis RT3S, AnyTone AT-D868UVE/D878UV/D578UV,
BTECH DMR-6x2 (PRO) and DM-1701, Baofeng DM-32UV, and the OpenGD77
firmware family.

Why Factory: the manufacturers ship device- and even revision-specific
CPS tools with no Linux build, so without qdmr these radios cannot be
programmed from openSUSE. Factory has chirp for analog/FM radios but
nothing that writes a DMR codeplug (qdmr reads chirp CSV). The
package has been maintained in hardware:sdr since 2021, tracks upstream
closely, and Fedora, Debian, Gentoo and NixOS all ship the same 0.15.1.

Upstream's test suite runs in %check (27/27 pass). rpmlint is clean
(0 errors, 0 warnings, 0 badness).</comment>
